## Initialization and recovery

Configure `InitOptions` before the constructor/init call in Quick Start. A nonzero `parameters_present` or `vars_present` field selects the corresponding value and bypasses its DSL initializer. Omitted parameters and persistent values use DSL defaults. Cold reinitialization starts a new run and clears installed callbacks; reinstall them before the next cycle. Parameters cannot be changed during a run.

To preset control/output, insert this fragment before the initialization call in Quick Start. Each presence flag selects that value; model execution may then overwrite it. Leave a flag zero to keep the DSL initializer.

<!-- example:initial-values -->

```cpp
{% if model.persistent_variables %}{% for item in model.persistent_variables.values() %}options.vars.{{ item.name | to_c_identifier }} = {{ 0 if item.name in model.forced_variables else 1 }};
options.vars_present.{{ item.name | to_c_identifier }} = 1;
{% endfor %}{% else %}/* This model has no persistent variables to preset. */
{% endif %}
```

### Hot Start

This fragment continues after a successful cycle in Quick Start. It captures {% if model.has_regions %}the configuration (the position of the machine and of every region), {% else %}the state, {% endif %}all persistent values and parameters from the same checkpoint and restores them without replaying entry actions. For saved checkpoints, supply those saved values instead. Do not replace checkpoint parameters with current defaults or new configuration. A composite target starts in initial-transition wait mode. Hot start preserves installed providers and hooks; a failed hot start preserves the previous run. Serialize snapshot capture and restoration with all other access to the instance.{% if model.has_regions %} A model with regions has no `current_state_id()`; capture the position with `get_configuration` and restore it with `hot_start_at`. `get_configuration` fails once the machine has ended. The plain `hot_start` still accepts the id of one state outside every region.{% else %} `get_configuration` and `hot_start_at` restore the same checkpoint from a configuration value; see [Configuration values](#configuration).{% endif %}{% set hidden_ns = namespace(found=false) %}{% for item in model.persistent_variables.values() %}{% if item.name[:2] == '__' %}{% set hidden_ns.found = true %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% if hidden_ns.found %}

The variables whose names start with `__` are generated by the model conversion for forced transitions, `active()` queries and similar constructs. They are ordinary variables of the model: copy them with the rest of `Vars` and take them from the saved checkpoint, so that they agree with the saved position.{% if model.forced_variables %} The `__forced_` variables belong to forced transitions: the machine uses them only inside a cycle, so they are `0` at every checkpoint. States named `__forced_gate_<n>` are generated pseudo states that a cycle only passes through.{% endif %}{% endif %}

<!-- example:hot-start -->

```cpp
{% if model.has_regions %}Wrapper::Configuration configuration;
Wrapper::Vars initial_vars = *machine.vars();
Wrapper::Parameters parameters;
memset(&parameters, 0, sizeof(parameters));
{% for item in model.parameters.values() %}parameters.{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }} = machine.get_param_{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}();
{% endfor %}
if (!machine.get_configuration(&configuration)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Cannot restore a completed machine as a live checkpoint\n");
    return 3;
}
if (!machine.hot_start_at(configuration, initial_vars, parameters)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "hot start failed: %s\n", machine.last_error());
    return 3;
}
{% else %}Wrapper::StateId state = machine.current_state_id();
Wrapper::Vars initial_vars = *machine.vars();
Wrapper::Parameters parameters;
memset(&parameters, 0, sizeof(parameters));
{% for item in model.parameters.values() %}parameters.{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }} = machine.get_param_{{ item.name | readonly_value_identifier }}();
{% endfor %}
if (state == {{ a }}_INVALID_STATE_ID) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Cannot restore a completed machine as a live checkpoint\n");
    return 3;
}
if (!machine.hot_start(state, initial_vars, parameters)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "hot start failed: %s\n", machine.last_error());
    return 3;
}
{% endif %}```

<a id="configuration"></a>

### Configuration values

A `Wrapper::Configuration` is a plain value that says where the machine is: `at` is the deepest active state outside every region, and `regions[r]` holds the deepest active state of region `r`, or `{{ a }}_REGION_SLOT_PENDING` while the region waits for its initial transition, or `{{ a }}_REGION_SLOT_DONE` once it has finished. A zeroed configuration is the root state before its own initialization. Copy it freely; a valid configuration has exactly one representation, so `memcmp` compares two configurations. `operator==` and `operator!=` compare two configurations directly. `validate_configuration` accepts or refuses a value and fills `Wrapper::ConfigurationError` with a `kind` (one of the `{{ a }}_CONFIGURATION_ERROR_*` macros), a byte `position` and a `message`; its error argument may be omitted.

The queries are static wrapper methods and need no machine: `region_status` answers `{{ a }}_REGION_STATUS_INACTIVE`, `_PENDING`, `_LIVE` or `_DONE` for a region id, `is_active` tells whether a state or one of its descendants is active, and `leaves` writes the active leaf ids and returns how many there are (call it with a `NULL` buffer and capacity `0` to count them). {% if model.has_regions %}Region ids are listed in the [Region IDs](#region-ids) table.{% else %}This model has no regions, so every configuration has the single position `at`.{% endif %}

The text form is the one the pyfcstm simulator prints and accepts, for example `Root.Run{Motion: Move, Guard: [done]}`: the path of the deepest state outside every region, then a braced block per region owner that lists each region with the path of its deepest state relative to that region. `[init]` marks a region that has not run its initial transition and `[done]` a region that has finished. `format_configuration` writes the text and its terminating NUL into your buffer, truncates to the size you give, and returns the full length (so a result of `size` or more means truncation); an invalid configuration writes an empty string. `parse_configuration` returns 1 and fills the configuration, or returns 0, fills the error and leaves the configuration unchanged; regions left out of the text are pending. Define `PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_CONFIGURATION_TEXT` for `machine.c`, `machine.cpp` and every consumer to leave out `format_configuration`, `parse_configuration` and their name tables; the value type, the validator and the queries stay, and `message` is then empty.

This fragment continues after a successful cycle in Quick Start. It prints the position, parses it back and checks that the round trip gives the same value. Pass a parsed configuration to `hot_start_at` together with the variables and parameters of the same checkpoint to resume from saved text.

<!-- example:configuration -->

```cpp
Wrapper::Configuration current;
Wrapper::Configuration parsed;
Wrapper::ConfigurationError parse_error;
char text[512];
size_t length;
if (!machine.get_configuration(&current)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "The machine has ended and has no configuration\n");
    return 4;
}
length = Wrapper::format_configuration(current, text, sizeof(text));
if (length >= sizeof(text)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "The configuration text needs %lu bytes\n", (unsigned long)(length + 1u));
    return 4;
}
printf("configuration = %s\n", text);
if (!Wrapper::parse_configuration(text, &parsed, &parse_error)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "configuration error %d at byte %lu: %s\n", parse_error.kind, (unsigned long)parse_error.position, parse_error.message);
    return 4;
}
printf("round trip equal = %d\n", memcmp(&parsed, &current, sizeof(parsed)) == 0);
```

## Results, failures and lifetime

Initialization and cycle return 1 on success and 0 on failure; inspect `last_error()` immediately after failure. A C++ constructor reports failure through `last_error()` instead of throwing. Sampling/validation/execution errors do not commit persistent values or `last_inputs`. Fix the cause and retry a cycle; it acquires fresh input. External reads, logs and action side effects are not rolled back. A successful Delta cycle holds persistent state and publishes its input snapshot.

The search that decides one whole cycle before it runs is a faithful port of the simulator's, with the same limits: nesting depth 64, group nesting 64 and 1000 steps. A failed cycle reports one of these categories in `last_error()`:

| Message starts with | Cause | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Speculative search exceeded the capacity safety limit` | The search needed more room than `{{ a }}_ARENA_CAPACITY` (configurations or work items) or `{{ a }}_TRAIL_CAPACITY` (nested checks); the message names the macro | Define the named macro to a larger value everywhere; see [Capacity macros](#capacity) |
| `Speculative DFS exceeded the structural stack-depth safety limit`, `Group initialization nested deeper than the structural safety limit` or `Speculative DFS exceeded the step safety limit` | The model nests, or chains pseudo states and transitions, beyond the fixed simulator limits | These limits are not configurable; remove the unbounded chain from the model |
| `Cannot reach a stoppable state in the same cycle; suspected loop.` | A closed loop through pseudo states or re-entry never reaches a state where the cycle can stop; this is an error, not a Delta cycle | Add an exit to a stoppable leaf, a terminating guard, or separate the events that drive exit and re-entry |
| `Invalid event id`, `Machine is not initialized` or `Invalid state id` | The call itself is wrong | Fix the caller |
| `Missing input provider`, `Input provider failed` or `must be finite` | Input sampling or a supplied value failed | Fix the provider or value and retry |

Configuration text and values report their own failures through `Wrapper::ConfigurationError` (see [Configuration values](#configuration)), not through `last_error()`.

Read control/output through `vars()` after success. This returns instance-owned storage, not a historical copy; copy `Vars` to retain values. Input getters are valid only during a cycle; read `last_inputs()` afterwards and copy `Inputs` when retaining history. Context pointers are valid only inside their callback. Getter calls require an initialized live instance. Calls after termination do not sample inputs. The wrapper owns its native instance by value; do not pass its `native_handle()` to a C destroy function.

